1) Starting
-----------

Double-click !Journal in the Apps directory. Its icon, a red notebook
with a pen, appears on the right of the icon bar.

  SELECT on the icon     today's page, ready for you to type
  ADJUST on the icon     the calendar
  MENU on the icon       Info, Today, Calendar, Find..., Password,
                         Choices..., Quit

If you have written on this date in another year, the Journal shows
you what you wrote, in an "On this day" window, when it starts
(Choices can turn this off).


2) Writing a page
-----------------

A page's title bar has its date: "Monday 28th September 2026". Just
type: the text wraps to the window, and the scroll bar moves through a
long day. Make the window bigger or smaller and the text follows.

At the top of the page:

  Mood       how you felt. Click the button beside it for a menu:
             Happy, Excited, Content, Calm, Tired, Anxious, Sad,
             Angry, or None.
  Weather    Sunny, Cloudy, Rain, Snow, Windy, Fog, Storm or None
  words      how many words there are on the page

Keys:

  Ctrl-T                start a new entry: a line with the time now
                        (such as 21:40), then the text under it. Use
                        it each time you come back to the day.
  Ctrl-D                type today's date
  F3                    save the page now
  F4                    Find (section 4)
  F5                    Go to: open the page for a date you type
  Print                 print the page
  Ctrl-Shift-Left       turn to the day before
  Ctrl-Shift-Right      turn to the day after
  Ctrl-F2               close the page
  F8 / F9               undo / redo
  Ctrl-C, Ctrl-X        copy, cut the selected text (to your
                        computer's clipboard)
  Ctrl-V                paste
  Arrows, Home, Copy    move about; Shift-Left and Shift-Right select
  Page Up, Page Down    a windowful up or down

With the mouse, drag across text to select it, double-click a word to
select it, and click ADJUST to make the selection bigger or smaller.

MENU on a page:

  Page      Info (words, characters, entries, your writing streak),
            Save, Export (the page as a text file or a web page),
            Print
  Edit      Undo, Redo, Cut, Copy, Select all
  Insert    Time, Date, and Prompt: a list of things to start you
            off when it's hard to know what to write ("Three good
            things about today:", "Something I learnt today:", ...)
  Mood, Weather
  Previous day, Next day, Calendar, Go to..., Find..., On this day

Drag a text file onto a page to put its text in where the caret is.
Drag one onto the Journal's icon to add it to the end of today's page.


3) The calendar
---------------

ADJUST on the Journal's icon (or Calendar on its menu) opens the
calendar at this month. Today has a red box round it. A day you have
written on has its number in bold, with a little face for its mood
and a picture of its weather; if it has neither, a few lines show
there is writing on it.

  Click a day                 open its page
  The arrow buttons           the month before / after (ADJUST goes
                              the other way)
  Left / Right arrow keys     the month before / after
  Up / Down arrow keys        a year back / on
  Today                       back to this month, and today's page

Under the calendar is how many days you have written on this month,
and your writing streak: how many days in a row you have written, up
to today (or yesterday, if you haven't written today yet).

MENU on the calendar has Today, Go to... (F5), Find... (F4), On this
day, Export and Print month (section 6).


4) Finding things again
-----------------------

Find... (F4, from a page, the calendar or the icon's menu) opens the
Find window. Type some words and press Return (or click Find): every
place they appear is listed, with the words around them. Capitals
don't matter, and moods and weather are found too ("rain" finds rainy
days). Double-click a line to open that day, with the words selected.

On this day (from a page's or the calendar's menu) lists what you
wrote on the same date in other years: "1 year ago", "2 years ago".
Double-click one to open it.

Go to... (F5) asks for a date, such as 25/12/2026, and opens its page.


5) Where the pages are kept
---------------------------

The journal is a folder on the hard disc, $.Journal to start with,
made the first time you write something. In it is a folder for each
year, in that one for each month, and in that a Text file for each
day you have written on:

  $.Journal.2026.09.28          Monday 28th September 2026

The file starts with the mood and weather, if the day has them:

  Mood: Happy
  Weather: Sunny

  09:14
  Went for a walk by the river ...

You can read and change these files in !Edit. If you change one while
the Journal is running it notices, and shows the new text (if you
have been typing on that page at the same time, it asks which you
want to keep). A page with nothing on it isn't kept: its file is
deleted, and so is its month's folder when that is empty.

To keep a second journal (for a holiday, say), drag any folder onto
the Journal's icon: the Journal uses it from then on. Choices... says
which folder the Journal uses when it starts.


6) Saving, exporting and printing
---------------------------------

Pages save themselves: a moment after you stop typing, when you turn
to another day, when you close them, and when the computer is shut
down. While a page has changes that haven't been saved yet, its title
ends with a *. (Choices can turn this off: then F3 saves a page, and
closing one with changes asks whether to Discard them, Cancel, or
Save them - as closing a document does.)

Export, on the calendar's menu, saves in other forms. Each opens a
Save box: drag its icon to a directory display, or type a full name
and click OK.

  Month as text           the month's days, one after another
  Month as web page       the same as a web page (read it in !Browse)
  Month as Draw file      a calendar page for the month, A4 size, with
                          the start of each day written in it, its
                          mood and its weather - print it from !Draw,
                          or put it into another document
  Journal as text         every day in the journal
  Journal as web page

A page's own menu can export just that page, as text or a web page.

Print (the Print key, or Print on a menu) prints a page, or the
calendar's month as you see it. Load !Printers (in Apps) first.


7) Locking the journal with a password
--------------------------------------

Password > Lock journal... on the icon's menu asks you to choose a
password and type it twice. Every page is then scrambled with it
(with AES encryption, done by your web browser), so nobody can read
the journal without the password: not in !Edit, and not by looking at
the disc. Locked pages have the Journal's own file type (a page with a
padlock on it); double-clicking one opens it in the Journal. A file
called Lock in the journal's folder tells the Journal that the
journal is locked, and whether a password is right: the password
itself isn't kept anywhere.

The next time the journal is opened, the Journal asks for the
password before it shows anything.

  Change password...    choose a new one (you type the old one first)
  Remove password...    make the pages ordinary Text files again

Keep the password safe. If it is forgotten, the pages can't be read
again - by anyone, and that includes you.


8) Choices
----------

Choices... on the icon's menu:

  Journal                     the folder the journal is kept in
  Save pages as I type        autosave (on to start with)
  Show On this day at start   the look back when the Journal starts
  Text font, Text size        how pages are written: Homerton,
                              Trinity, Corpus or the System font

Set uses them until the Journal quits; Save keeps them for next time
too (in Choices:Journal); Default puts back the ones it started with.


9) Things to know
-----------------

  - Ctrl-T is also "new tab" in some web browsers on Windows and
    Linux, which keep it for themselves. If it opens a tab instead,
    use Insert > Time on the page's menu.
  - Pages are Latin-1 text, as RISC OS's are: characters that RISC OS
    doesn't have are typed, and saved, as "?".
  - The hard disc is kept in your web browser's storage (see the
    README). *ResetDisc, or clearing the browser's data for this
    site, removes the journal with everything else you have saved.
  - Only one Journal runs at a time. Double-clicking a locked page
    while it runs opens the page in the one that is running.
  - !Organiser (in Examples.JSApps) has a diary of appointments; the
    Journal is for writing.
